GHOST code: Geophysical High Order Suite for Turbulence
=======================================================

The code and different solvers provided here numerically integrate 
several PDEs and particles in periodic domains. Several solvers are
provided (see the examples directory for different input files to
integrate the different models available). A pseudo-spectral method 
is used to compute spatial derivatives, while different time steppers
of adjustable order can be used to evolve the system in the time
domain. To compile, you need the FFTW library installed on your
system (or other supported FFT libraries with compatible interfaces)
and some flavor of MPI. Note that only serial transforms in the FFT
packages are used, since many MPI versions of FFT libraries implement
blocking communications. Instead, a custom non-blocking parallel FFT
(FFTP) based on FFTW is used instead.

1. Installing FFTW
==================

The default compiling options in GHOST assume FFTW 3.x is installed
in your system. FFTW can be downloaded from https://www.fftw.org/.
You need both single and double precision libraries for GHOST. To
compile FFTW, download the source files, 'cd' to the directory
containing the source and type

-------------------------------------------------------------------
./configure --enable-openmp --enable-threads
make install
make distclean
./configure --enable-openmp --enable-threads --enable-float
make
make install
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The first pass installs the double precision libraries, and the 
second pass installs the single precision libraries. By default, 
the libraries are installed in '/usr/local/lib', '/usr/local/man', 
etc. You can specify an installation prefix other than '/usr/local' 
(e.g. the user's home directory) by giving `configure' the option 
'--prefix=PATH'.

FFTW uses GCC by default if GNU compilers are present. To use 
other compilers, you can pass variables to the configure script. 
In a Bourne-compatible shell, you can do that on the command line 
like this:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
CC=pgcc CFLAGS='-fastsse' FC=pgf90 ./configure
-------------------------------------------------------------------

CFLAGS passes flags to the C compiler, and FC sets the Fortran 
compiler (use the 'env' command at the beginning of this line in
C-like shells).

2. Installing MPI
=================

GHOST needs some flavor of MPI to be installed in your system. If
not provided by your operating system, you can download OpenMPI
from https://www.open-mpi.org/, and follow the instructions there
for installation.

3. Compilation
==============

3.1. Setting code options:

Most users can skip this section and move on to compilation in Sec.
3.2. Basic compiling options are set in GHOST/UserConfig.cmake:

# ----------------------- Options / variants ----------------------
option(P_HYBRID "Enable hybrid OpenMP"       OFF)
set(PRECISION "SINGLE" CACHE STRING "Precision (SINGLE or DOUBLE)")
set(FFTP "fftp-3"      CACHE STRING "FFT library (fftp-3, fftp-gpu, fftp-mkl)")
set(IOLIB "mpiio"      CACHE STRING "IO library (mpiio)")
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The most relevant option for users is PRECISION, which can be set
to SINGLE or DOUBLE. FFTP can be used to change the underlying FFT
library used at compile time. Note that GHOST 3.x now only supports
mpi I/O for binary files.

The remaining options in this file are optional and the defaults give
good performance in most computers. But if needed, you can set three
more parameters for optimization purposes (again, the default values
work fine in most computers, don't change the values if you are unsure
about the meaning of these variables). The lines

# ----------------------- Advanced set up params ------------------
set(IKIND 8  CACHE STRING "Integer kind")
set(CSIZE 8  CACHE STRING "Cache size")
set(NSTRIP 1 CACHE STRING "Strip mining")
-------------------------------------------------------------------

set the variables 'ikind', 'csize', and 'nstrip' used in parallel
FFTs. 'IKIND' should be ketp at 8. 'CSIZE' is used by the parallel
FFT to do cache-friendly transpositions. The optimal value for a
particular machine can be adjusted doing benchmarks, but rule of
thumb values are 8 if the L1 cache is smaller or equal to 64 kb,
and 16 if the L1 cache is larger than 64 kb (24 or 32 may be best
in some processors). The variable 'NSTRIP' controls strip mining
during the transpositions. The default value is 1 and in that case
no strip mining is done.

3.2 Compiling the code:

If all libraries and compilers are defined in your path, you can
compile just by doing
 
-------------------------------------------------------------------
cd GHOST/build
cmake ..
make
-------------------------------------------------------------------

This will create a GHOST executable file in GHOST/bin.

If CMake cannot find your libraries, or if you want to use specific
compilers or library paths, you can set them by doing:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
cd GHOST/build
CC=gcc FC=gfortran cmake .. -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/foo/openmpi \
  -DFFTW3_ROOT=/foo/fftw
make
-------------------------------------------------------------------

where /foo/openmpi is the path to the MPI libraries, and /foo/fftw
is the path to the FFTW libraries.

3.3 Parallelization models

By default, GHOST uses 1D domain decomposition with pure MPI 
parallelization. If hybrid parallelization was enabled at compile
time, the number of MPI jobs and the number of threads in each job
can be set independently at run time. To define the number of
threads (in this example, set to 4 threads) in a Bourne-like shell,
set the environment variable 

-------------------------------------------------------------------
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=4
-------------------------------------------------------------------

and in a C-like shell do

-------------------------------------------------------------------
setenv OMP_NUM_THREADS 4
-------------------------------------------------------------------

For the hybrid code to scale properly, processor affinity and/or 
binding are crucial. How to set processor affinity and binding is 
platform dependent; check the platform documentation where you are 
running for more information.

3.3.1 GPU offload

GHOST can offload the field computations to GPUs with OpenMP target
directives. The build is selected with

-------------------------------------------------------------------
cmake .. -DP_GPU=ON -DGPU_VENDOR=AMD -DGPU_ARCH=gfx90a
cmake .. -DP_GPU=ON -DGPU_VENDOR=NVIDIA -DGPU_ARCH=cc80
-------------------------------------------------------------------

P_GPU implies P_HYBRID (the host parts of the code stay threaded).
The offload build needs a compiler with OpenMP offload support for
Fortran: nvfortran (NVIDIA GPUs of compute capability 7.0 or newer,
GPU_ARCH=ccXX) or flang (AMD GPUs with the ROCm flang, GPU_ARCH=gfxXXX;
NVIDIA GPUs with an upstream LLVM flang built with the NVPTX backend,
GPU_ARCH=sm_XX). gfortran builds the host versions only. The MPI
library must be built with the same compiler and, for performance,
must be GPU-aware (UCX with the ROCm or CUDA transports). The vendor
runtime is found through the ROCM_PATH or CUDA_HOME environment
variables (or -DROCM_PATH=, -DCUDA_PATH=).

At run time GHOST binds each MPI task to the GPU number
rank modulo (GPUs per node), so the number of tasks per node must be
a multiple of the number of GPUs per node (one task per GPU is the
intended configuration). All field arrays (states, workspace,
wavenumbers) are allocated through src/memmgt/gmem_mod.f90, which
creates a device copy of every array; the fields live on the device
during the time step and their host copies are refreshed only for
output. See src/memmgt/gdevice_mod.f90 for the details. The kernels
are written twice, as OpenMP target regions for the offload build and
as threaded loops for the host build, selected with
#if defined(GHOST_GPU). For the moment only the HD solver (with
passive scalars) runs on the device; particles are not supported in
offload builds.

The FFTs of the offload build are computed by the fftp-gpu backend
(src/fftp-gpu, selected automatically with P_GPU): cuFFT or hipFFT on
the device, with the slab exchange done by packing the blocks on the
device into contiguous messages handed to a GPU-aware MPI library.
The host path of the same backend uses FFTW, so the FFTs of the
initial conditions and of the diagnostics, which run on the host
copies, are the same as in fftp-3. If the MPI library is not
GPU-aware, set the environment variable GHOST_GPU_EXCHANGE=staged to
move the exchange buffers through the host (slower). The transforms
are unnormalized and produce the same layout as fftp-3, so the two
backends are interchangeable; results differ only by round-off.

With ROCm, GPU-aware MPI performs best with OMPI_MCA_pml=ucx and
UCX_TLS=rocm_copy,rocm_ipc,sm,self.

Note for flang builds: flang places automatic arrays on the stack
whatever their size, and the diagnostic routines (energy, spectra,
etc.) still declare field-sized automatic arrays, so runs at large
resolution need an unlimited stack size (ulimit -s unlimited, or the
equivalent in the batch script).

3.4 About the solvers:

Solvers, with their equations, output, and definitions, are defined
in GHOST/src/pdes (for fluids) and GHOST/src/particles (for
particles). Each solver has its own file, with its own methods
(e.g., the incompressible Navier-Stokes equations are defined in
GHOST/src/pdes/hd_solver.f90). See each solver file for more
information.

4. Running the code
===================

GHOST can be executed using mpirun or the correspondent vendor
equivalent. The first node then reads the parameters for the run 
from the input file, and passes the values to the rest of the 
nodes. 

4.1. Input file:

The input file for all the solvers is named 'parameter.inp'. The 
file is separated into several 'namelists', which have the following 
syntax:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
! General comments
&listname1
variable1 = 1.00 ! Comment
variable2 = 2.00 ! Another comment
/

! More comments
&listname2
variable3 = 3.00 ! Comment
variable4 = 4.00 ! Another comment
/
...
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The number of namelists required depends on the solver, initial
conditions, and forcing methods selected. In GHOST/src/examples,
there are several examples of input files to run GHOST with
different solvers and configurations. As an example, to do a
simulation with the incompressible Navier-Stokes solver and without
particles, you can copy GHOST/src/examples/parameter.hd into
parameter.inp:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
cd GHOST/bin
cp ../src/examples/parameter.hd parameter.inp
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The order of the namelists in the file 'parameter.inp', and 
the order of the variables inside each list, are not important. All 
solvers need at least a namelist 'grid' whith parameters

-------------------------------------------------------------------
! Grid resolution
&grid
nx = 64
ny = 64
nz = 64
/
-------------------------------------------------------------------

This sets the spatial resolution. The namelist 'boxparams' may not
be present, in that case the simulation is performed in a (2 pi)^3
periodic domain. If present, Lx, Ly, and Lz set the domain lengths
in units of 2 pi:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
&boxparams
Lx = 1.00        ! Length of the box in x
Ly = 1.00        ! Length of the box in y
Lz = 1.00        ! Length of the box in z
-------------------------------------------------------------------

All solvers require a 'status' namelist:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
&status
&status
idir = "outs"
odir = "outs"
stat = 0         ! last binary file if restarting an old run
mult = 1         ! decreases the time step
bench = 0        ! benchmark mode if 1
outs = 0         ! controls the amount of binary output
dt = 1e-3        ! time step
step = 501       ! total number of steps
tstep = 250      ! number of steps between binary output
sstep = 250      ! number of steps between spectrum output
cstep = 5        ! number of steps between writing global quantities
cort = 0.2       ! global correlation time for random forcing methods
seed = 1000      ! global seed for random number generators
/
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The most relevant variables in this namelist are:

idir : directory for binary input
odir : directory for binary output
stat : the number of the last binary file available if a 
       simulation is restarted. If zero, a new simulation is done.
       If not zero, previous outputs are read if the initial
       conditions to the fields are set to "read" (otherwise that
       field can be reseted to a different state by the user).
mult : time step multiplier, should be 1 in most cases (if 
       larger than one, the time step is divided by multiplier, 
       and all 'step' variables multiplied by this variable).
bench: if >=1, performs a benchmark run.
outs : controls the amount of output written in the binary files.
dt   : sets the time step, which must satisfy the CFL condition 
       dt <= 1/(u*n), where u is a characteristic speed, and n is 
       the linear resolution.
step : controls the total number of time steps the code will do.
tstep, sstep, and cstep: number of time steps done between 
       exporting global quantities (cstep), energy spectra (sstep), 
       and binary files (tstep).
cort : correlation time for random forcing methods/random processes.
seed : seed for the random number generator.

All runs also read a namelist with parameters for the time stepper: 

-------------------------------------------------------------------
! Time stepping method
&stepper
sname  = "TRADITIONAL" ! TRADITIONAL or GEXRK
norder = 2             ! order
nstage = 2             ! number of stages
/
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The variables in this list are:

sname : The time stepper (TRADITIONAL: a low storage RK method that
        saves memory and time, of second order or of order=norder
	only for linear problems; GEXRK: Explicity RK of order [1,4])
norder, nstage: the order of the method (norder=stage for the moment)

The PDE solver is chosen with the 'Solver' namelist:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
! Solver
&pdes
solver = "HD"    ! Solver name, reads a namelist with the same name
/
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Each solver reads a namelist with its own name. For incompressible
Navier-Stokes (HD) the parameters are:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
! HD solver specific parameters
&HD
nu  = 3.e-3      ! kinematic viscosity
dorot = .FALSE.  ! do rotation
omegax = 0.0     ! rotation in x (ignored if dorot is FALSE)
omegay = 0.0     ! rotation in y (ignored if dorot is FALSE)
omegaz = 0.0     ! rotation in z (ignored if dorot is FALSE)
npassive = 0     ! number of passive scalars
spectlod = 1     ! spectral output level of detail [1-3]
/
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Finally, initial conditions and forcing are chosen with their own
namelists:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
! Initial conditions. List separated by ';' to set up ICs.
! For v, ICs can be: read_v, null_v, tg_v, abc_v, random_v
! Use "read_v" if restarting from a previous file with stat > 0
! If stat > 0 and the ICs are not "read_v", GHOST resets the flow.
&initial_conditions
iclist = "null_v"
/

! Forcing. List separated by ';', each forcing is a pair of
! methods separated by ',': an initial forcing, and an update scheme
! Forcings for v: null_fv, tg_fv, abc_fv, random_fv
! Updates for fv: constant_fv, shift_fv, shuffle_fv
&forcing
forces = "tg_fv,constant_fv"
/

! IC and forcing specific parameters, in namelists with their names:
! Parameters for Taylor-Green velocity forcing
&tg_fv
f0 = 0.37714265  ! amplitude of the mechanical forcing
kdn = 1.00       ! minimum wavenumber
kup = 2.00       ! maximum wavenumbe
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Note that each initial condition or forcing method may require its
own namelist. 'null_v', that sets the initial velocity to zero, does
not have parameters and as a result does not have an associated
namelist.

Similar lists are used for solvers with active scalars, magnetic
fields, wave functions, particles, etc. See the examples in
'GHOST/src/examples' for more details.

4.3. Running:

How to run GHOST depends on the system. A serial (no parallelization)
run can be done just by doing:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
./GHOST
-------------------------------------------------------------------

in the directory where the executable is. To run in parallel, e.g.,
using 4 MPI tasks, do:

-------------------------------------------------------------------
mpirun -np 4 ./GHOST
-------------------------------------------------------------------

4.3. Output files:

When the code is not running in benchmark mode, it writes several 
files with global quantities, spectra, and field components. Global
quantities and spectra are written to text files in the directory
where the binary was executed (this default behavior can be changed
in the input file), while binary files are written to the directory
defined in the 'parameter.inp' file.

Binary files are named by the field name and a number that labels the
time snapshot. As an example, the x-component of the velocity field
in a hydrodynamic simulation is stored as 'vx.NNN.out', where NNN
is proportional to the time at which the snapshot was taken. The
actual time can be computed as t = dt*tstep*(NNN-1). Examples on how
to read these binary files (as well as particles binary files) are
given in GHOST/contrib.

Energy spectra and other spectral quantities are written to files
named  'kspectrum.MMMM.txt', 'mspectrum.MMMM.txt', and so on. Spectra 
of kinetic or magnetic helicity are written to files
'khelicity.MMMM.txt' and 'mhelicity.MMMM.txt'. Extra files with 
the energy transfer function, or the spectrum in the parallel and 
perpendicular directions (e.g., to the axis of rotation), are also 
written by some solvers. Here, MMMM is proportional to the time at
which the spectra were computed, and the actual time is given by
t = dt*sstep*(MMMM-1).

The code also writes text files with quantities integrated over all 
volume (e.g., 'helicity.txt', 'balance.txt', etc.). When the external
force is constant, these files can be used to verify consistency of
the code when doing a simulation in a new machine. In a hydrodynamic
run, the file 'balance.txt' has four columns with the time (t), two
times the energy (2.E), two times the enstrophy (2.Omega), and the
energy injection rate (eps). These quantities must satisfy the
relation

dE/dt = -2.nu.Omega+eps

where the time derivative of the energy can be computed using 
centered finite differences.

5. References:

The main reference for GHOST, with a detailed description of the
parallelization scheme, is: P.D. Mininni, D.L. Rosenberg, R. Reddy,
and A. Pouquet, Parallel Computing 37(6), 316 (2011).

The GPU implementation is described in: D. Rosenberg, P.D. Mininni,
R. Reddy and A. Pouquet, Atmospheres 11, 178 (2020).

We ask users to cite at least the first paper when referencing
GHOST. If GPUs are used, please also cite the second paper.